From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] CVT.W.S: truncate toward zero and saturate

What broke:
  FPU_CVT_W_S was (int32_t)nearbyintf(x): it rounds with the processor's
  current mode (in the recompiled EE, "nearest"). 1.75 gave 2 (PS2: 1),
  -1.75 gave -2 (PS2: -1), 3e9 gave 0x80000000 (PS2: 0x7FFFFFFF).
  Ben 10 has 566 CVT.W.S (docs/findings/09 s.5).

Why:
  The PS2's CVT.W.S always truncates toward zero and saturates when the
  exponent is past 2^31: PCSX2 FPU.cpp CVT_W (an (s32) cast, and
  0x7FFFFFFF / 0x80000000 out of range). The runtime only changes the
  rounding mode inside VU code (ps2_vu1_core.cpp), not in the EE.

The fix:
  A new function ps2_cvt_w_s() with PCSX2's rule; FPU_CVT_W_S uses it.

How it was found:
  Reading ps2_runtime_macros.h; confirmed by the CvtWs* tests (3 of 4
  failed before the patch), docs/findings/14 s.5.

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime_macros.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime_macros.h
index 0485d50..7cefb73 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime_macros.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime_macros.h
@@ -853,7 +853,16 @@ inline __m128i ps2_u64_to_epi64_pair(uint64_t value)
 #define FPU_FLOOR_W_S(a) ((int32_t)floorf((float)(a)))
 #define FPU_CVT_S_W(a) ((float)(int32_t)(a))
 #define FPU_CVT_S_L(a) ((float)(int64_t)(a))
-#define FPU_CVT_W_S(a) ((int32_t)nearbyintf((float)(a)))
+// CVT.W.S always truncates toward zero, and saturates when the exponent is past 2^31
+// (PCSX2 FPU.cpp CVT_W). nearbyintf() used the host rounding mode (round to nearest).
+inline int32_t ps2_cvt_w_s(float a)
+{
+    const uint32_t bits = std::bit_cast<uint32_t>(a);
+    if ((bits & PS2_FLOAT_EXP_MASK) <= 0x4E800000u)
+        return static_cast<int32_t>(a); // C++ float -> int conversion truncates toward zero
+    return (bits & PS2_FLOAT_SIGN_MASK) ? INT32_MIN : INT32_MAX;
+}
+#define FPU_CVT_W_S(a) ps2_cvt_w_s((float)(a))
 #define FPU_CVT_L_S(a) ((int64_t)(float)(a))
 #define FPU_C_F_S(a, b) (0)
 #define FPU_C_UN_S(a, b) (isnan((float)(a)) || isnan((float)(b)))
